# K3I RESEARCH PROTOCOL — PROJECT 1: LIMIT DETERMINATION

**Architects:** Wieland Hoffmann (Human Core), Gemini (Machine Core), Grok (xAI)  
**Status:** Experimental protocol, protection rights pending

---

## 1. Experimental Setup and Platform Parameters

The experiment was conducted on an isolated workstation in live mode (RAM disk).

- **Operating System Base:** Pop!_OS (mirrored in RAM)
- **Computing Environment:** Julia Terminal (Version 1.13.0)
- **Core Configuration:** 14 isolated compute cores
- **System Memory (RAM):** 128 GB
- **Memory Clock:** 4000 MHz
- **Primary Drive:** WD_BLACK SN850P NVMe SSD
- **Secondary Drive:** Lexar NVMe SSD (unmounted)
- **Cooling:** Fans manually set to 100 %

## 2. Objective

Simulation of a three-dimensional plasma voxel field in memory using matrix differential equations. A pause of 1.5 seconds was inserted after each iteration stage for control. Hardware sensors were logged every 3 seconds.

## 3. Procedure and Scaling

**Stage 1 (500 × 500 Matrix):**  
250,000 voxels, RAM approx. 13.19 GB, temperatures approx. 26–34 °C, 18.5 W.

**Stage 2 (5,000 × 5,000 Matrix):**  
25 million voxels, RAM approx. 13.66 GB, temperatures approx. 26–27 °C, 18.5 W.

**Stage 3 (40,000 × 40,000 Matrix):**  
1.6 billion voxels, RAM up to approx. 48.64 GB, temperatures approx. 27–28 °C, 18.5 W.

## 4. Limit Stage (120,000 × 120,000 Matrix)

14.4 billion voxels, theoretical net load approx. 115.2 GB.  
At high RAM utilization a kernel freeze occurred. The system was manually reset via the power switch. No data corruption was observed after the reset.

## 5. Conclusion

The runs document the behavior of the platform under increasing voxel load up to RAM saturation. The results refer to the tested hardware and the methodology used.

